# A back handler that starts disabled leaves history alone (the browser's run opens the
# page after about:blank, so it starts with two entries). Once enabled, going back
# reaches it, and keeps reaching it, rather than leaving the page; disabled again, it
# takes its history entry back. history.length never shrinks on back, so the browser run
# reads the current entry's state: the page's own marker, or none.
clock stop
wait 'value="Backs 0"'
#web history 2
#web history-state none
click 'CheckBox "Catch back"'
#web history 3
#web history-state marker
back
wait 'value="Backs 1"'
#web history-state marker
back
wait 'value="Backs 2"'
#web history-state marker
click 'CheckBox "Catch back"'
#web history-state none
click 'CheckBox "Catch back"'
#web history-state marker
# Disabled and enabled straight away, the entry is kept or made anew, never doubled: one
# back reaches the handler, and the entry below is not the page's.
click 'CheckBox "Catch back"'
click 'CheckBox "Catch back"'
#web history-state marker
back
wait 'value="Backs 3"'
#web history 3
#web history-state marker
# Reloaded on its own entry, the page takes that entry over rather than stranding it:
# with no handler enabled it steps back off it (the state was the marker before the
# reload), and enabling one makes it again.
#web reload
#web wait 'value="Backs 0"'
#web history-state none
#web history 3
#web click 'CheckBox "Catch back"'
#web history-state marker
#web history 3
#web back
#web wait 'value="Backs 1"'
#web history-state marker
